{"name":"Sweat Bee","hex":"#1d4e8f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweat bee","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various bees that are attracted to the salt in human sweat, principally Halictidae spp."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweat_bee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Halictidae","blocks":[{"text":"Halictidae is the second-largest family of bees with nearly 4,500 species. They are commonly called sweat bees, as they are often attracted to perspiration. Halictid species are an extremely diverse group that can vary greatly in appearance. These bees occur all over the world and are found on every continent except Antarctica. Usually dark-colored and often metallic, halictids are found in various sizes, colors and patterns. Several species are all or partly green and a few are red, purple, or blue. A number of them have yellow markings, especially the males, which commonly have yellow faces, a pattern widespread among the various families of bees. The family is one of many with short tongues and is best distinguished by the arcuate basal vein found on the wing. Females in this family tend to be larger than the males. They are the group for which the term eusocial was coined by entomologist Suzanne Batra."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg/330px-Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halictidae","wikidata":"Q2276661"},{"title":"Agapostemon","blocks":[{"text":"The genus Agapostemon is a common group of Western Hemisphere sweat bees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Agapostemon_texanus.jpg/330px-Agapostemon_texanus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agapostemon","wikidata":"Q3606354"},{"title":"Bee","blocks":[{"text":"Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species – including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees – are social insects living in colonies, whereas over 90% of bee species – including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees – are solitary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg/330px-Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee","wikidata":"Q7391"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg/500px-Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg/1280px-Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg/500px-Halictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHalictus_scabiosae_MHNT.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Systropha_planidens.jpg/500px-Systropha_planidens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Systropha_planidens.jpg/500px-Systropha_planidens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Systropha planidens, a specialist pollinator of Convolvulus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASystropha_planidens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Halictus_rubicundus_394603187.jpg/500px-Ha